g('how-customs-duty-is-calculated-in-india', 'customs', 'How customs duty is calculated in India|with a worked example',
  'How import duty works in India: assessable value, basic customs duty, social welfare surcharge, AIDC and IGST, with a worked example.',
  'India charges several duties on an import, one on top of another. The order matters, so here it is with numbers.',
  [('The order of calculation', ['1. Assessable value (AV): the CIF value, plus landing charges, converted at the customs exchange rate.', '2. Basic customs duty (BCD): a percentage of AV.', '3. Social welfare surcharge (SWS): usually 10 percent of the BCD, unless the product is exempt.', '4. Other duties where they apply, such as AIDC, anti-dumping or safeguard duty.', '5. IGST: charged on AV plus the duties above.']),
   ('Worked example', [('table', ['Step', 'Amount (Rs)'], [['Assessable value', '1,00,000'], ['BCD at 10 percent', '10,000'], ['SWS at 10 percent of BCD', '1,000'], ['IGST at 18 percent of 1,11,000', '19,980'], ['<b>Total duty</b>', '<b>30,980</b>']], 'Example only. Rates differ for each product and many have special notifications.')]),
   ('Why the effective rate is not just BCD plus IGST', 'IGST is charged on a value that already includes the duty, so the effective rate is higher than a simple addition. The surcharge adds a little more.'),
   ('Use the right inputs', 'Get the correct HS code, the true transaction value and the customs exchange rate for the week. A small error in any of the three changes the result.')],
  [('Is IGST on imports refundable?', 'A registered business can claim it as input tax credit, subject to the GST rules.'),
   ('Where do I find the BCD rate?', 'In the Customs Tariff for the eight-digit code and in notifications that give exemptions. Our calculator uses both.'),
   ('What about the exchange rate?', 'Customs uses a rate notified by CBIC, not the bank rate.')],
  ('customs-duty-calculator', 'Open the duty calculator'))
g('assessable-value-cif-landing-charges', 'customs', 'Assessable value for customs|CIF, freight, insurance and landing charges',
  'How the assessable value of an import is built: transaction value, freight, insurance and the 1 percent landing charge rule.',
  'Duty is charged on the assessable value, not on the invoice price alone. It adds the cost of getting the goods to India.',
  [('The building blocks', ['Transaction value: the price actually paid or payable.', 'Freight to the Indian port or airport.', 'Insurance to the Indian port or airport.', 'Landing charges: 1 percent of the CIF value, added by the valuation rules.', 'Other additions: commissions other than buying commission, royalties and licence fees tied to the sale, and the cost of packing and assists.']),
   ('When freight or insurance is not known', 'If freight is not shown, the rules use a standard estimate. For air shipments, freight is capped at a percentage of the FOB value. If insurance is not shown, a flat percentage of FOB is used. Check the current valuation rules.'),
   ('FOB, CIF and CFR invoices', 'On an FOB invoice you add freight and insurance. On CIF, they are already in the price. On CFR, you add insurance. Then you add the landing charge.'),
   ('Exchange rate', 'Use the CBIC notified import rate that is in force on the date of the bill of entry. It is published regularly and the rate differs from a bank rate.')],
  [('Is the landing charge on FOB or CIF?', 'It is 1 percent of the CIF value in the standard rule.'),
   ('Are discounts allowed?', 'Genuine, documented discounts at the time of sale can be allowed. Later or conditional discounts may not.'),
   ('Does inland freight in India count?', 'No. Charges after the Indian port of import are not part of the assessable value.')],
  ('landed-cost-calculator', 'Open the landed cost calculator'))
g('customs-valuation-methods-india', 'customs', 'Customs valuation methods|the six rules in order',
  'The six valuation methods in Indian customs, from transaction value to the fallback method, and when customs can reject your invoice price.',
  'Customs normally accepts the price you paid. When it does not, the valuation rules give a ladder of methods that it must follow in order.',
  [('The ladder', [('table', ['Rule', 'Method'], [['3', 'Transaction value of the goods'], ['4', 'Transaction value of identical goods'], ['5', 'Transaction value of similar goods'], ['7', 'Deductive value, from the resale price in India'], ['8', 'Computed value, from the cost of production'], ['9', 'Fallback, using reasonable means']], 'The importer can ask that Rule 8 be used before Rule 7.')]),
   ('When the invoice is rejected', 'Customs can doubt the declared value if it is much lower than similar imports, if the buyer and seller are related and the price is influenced, or if the documents are unclear. It has to ask you for proof first.'),
   ('How to protect yourself', ['Keep the contract, the purchase order, payment proof and the supplier price list.', 'Declare all additions: royalty, assists, commissions.', 'Respond fast and in writing to a value query, with the evidence.']),
   ('If you disagree', 'You can ask for a speaking order and appeal. Customs must give reasons for rejecting the transaction value.')],
  [('Can customs use a database price?', 'It can use a price of identical or similar goods, but only under the ladder and with proper reasons.'),
   ('What is a related party?', 'Companies under common control, or with a director or share link as defined in the rules.'),
   ('Does a higher value cost more duty?', 'Yes. Duty is a percentage of the value, so a rejection upward raises the duty.')],
  ('landed-cost-calculator', 'Open the landed cost calculator'))
g('royalty-licence-fees-assists-customs-value', 'customs', 'Royalty, licence fees and assists in customs value|what gets added',
  'When royalties, licence fees, commissions and assists are added to the customs value of an import in India.',
  'Some payments that are not on the invoice still belong in the customs value. Leaving them out can lead to a demand and penalty.',
  [('Royalty and licence fees', 'Payments that you must make as a condition of the sale of the imported goods, for example to use a brand or a patent in making or selling them, are added to the value.'),
   ('Assists', 'If you give the supplier materials, tools, moulds, designs or engineering for free or at a lower price, their value is added to the value of the goods.'),
   ('Commissions and other charges', ['Selling commission paid to the seller or an agent of the seller is added.', 'Buying commission paid to your own agent is not.', 'Packing and containers that are part of the goods are added.']),
   ('Related buyers and sellers', 'When the two are related, customs looks harder at the price and at the royalty arrangement. Keep transfer pricing papers and agreements.')],
  [('Is a software licence a royalty?', 'It can be, if it is a condition of the sale and tied to the goods. Get advice on each arrangement.'),
   ('Does a service fee count?', 'Not if it is for a separate service after import, but it depends on the contract.'),
   ('What if I only found out later?', 'Declare the addition as soon as you know. A voluntary payment is easier than a demand notice.')],
  ('landed-cost-calculator', 'Open the landed cost calculator'))
g('social-welfare-surcharge-explained', 'customs', 'Social welfare surcharge on imports|what it is',
  'What the social welfare surcharge on imports to India is, how it is calculated and when it does not apply.',
  'The social welfare surcharge (SWS) is a small additional levy that India charges on many imports. It is calculated on the basic customs duty, not on the goods.',
  [('How it is worked out', 'It is generally 10 percent of the basic customs duty. So if the BCD is Rs 10,000, SWS is Rs 1,000.'),
   ('Where it fits', 'It is added after BCD and before IGST. IGST is then charged on the value plus BCD plus SWS and other duties.'),
   ('Exemptions', 'Many goods are exempt or have a different treatment under notifications. The list has changed in recent budgets, so always check the current notification for your HS code.'),
   ('In the calculator', 'Our duty calculator applies SWS as 10 percent of the BCD unless an exemption is built into the data for your code.')],
  [('Is SWS the same as the health cess?', 'They are different levies with different rules. The calculator shows what applies.'),
   ('Does SWS apply to zero BCD goods?', 'If BCD is zero, SWS on it is zero too.'),
   ('Is SWS creditable?', 'No, it is a cost. IGST is the one that you can claim as credit.')],
  ('customs-duty-calculator', 'Open the duty calculator'))
g('igst-on-imports-and-input-tax-credit', 'customs', 'IGST on imports|and how to claim the credit',
  'How IGST is charged on imports into India, how the base is built and how a registered business claims input tax credit on it.',
  'Every import into India carries IGST, on top of the customs duties. For a GST registered business it is not a cost, because it comes back as credit.',
  [('How it is charged', 'IGST is charged on the assessable value plus the customs duties payable on the goods. The rate follows the product: 5, 12, 18 or 28 percent, or other rates the council sets, so check the current rate for your code.'),
   ('Claiming the credit', 'The bill of entry data flows to the GST portal. A registered importer claims the IGST paid as input tax credit when the goods are used for taxable supplies. Make sure the bill of entry carries your GSTIN.'),
   ('Cash flow', 'The IGST has to be paid up front, and the credit offsets later GST payments. For importers with slow sales this can tie up cash. A bonded warehouse can defer it.'),
   ('Common errors', 'A wrong GSTIN on the bill of entry, or goods imported in the name of one entity and sold by another, stops the credit.')],
  [('Is IGST paid on exports?', 'Exports are zero rated. IGST is not paid with an LUT, or it is refunded if paid.'),
   ('Can an unregistered buyer claim the credit?', 'No. Credit is only for registered persons.'),
   ('Where do I see the credit?', 'In your GST return data, which draws on the bill of entry details.')],
  ('landed-cost-calculator', 'Open the landed cost calculator'))
g('aidc-agriculture-infrastructure-development-cess', 'customs', 'AIDC on imports|Agriculture Infrastructure and Development Cess',
  'What the Agriculture Infrastructure and Development Cess (AIDC) is, which imports it applies to and how it fits into the duty calculation.',
  'AIDC is an extra cess on certain imports, for example some fuels, fertilisers and agricultural goods. It was introduced in the Union Budget of 2021.',
  [('What it is', 'A levy on specified goods, shown as a rate in the tariff or in a notification. In many cases the basic customs duty was reduced as AIDC was added, so the total change for the importer was small.'),
   ('How it is charged', 'It is a separate duty line, calculated on the assessable value or a quantity, depending on the entry. It is added before IGST, which then applies to the total.'),
   ('How to see it', 'The calculator shows AIDC for the HSN lines that carry it, together with BCD, SWS and IGST.'),
   ('Check the notification', 'AIDC rates and the list of goods are changed by notification. Do not rely on an old rate card.')],
  [('Is AIDC creditable?', 'AIDC is a cost. Only IGST is available as credit for a registered buyer.'),
   ('Does it apply to all farm goods?', 'No, only to the goods listed in the tariff and notifications.'),
   ('Is it the same as the old cess?', 'No. It is a different levy with its own legal basis.')],
  ('customs-duty-calculator', 'Open the duty calculator'))
g('anti-dumping-duty-explained', 'customs', 'Anti-dumping duty|what importers should know',
  'What anti-dumping duty is in India, how DGTR investigations work, how long the duty lasts and how to check whether it applies to your import.',
  'Anti-dumping duty is an extra duty on a product from a particular country or producer when it is sold in India below a fair price and hurts local industry.',
  [('How it starts', 'Indian producers file a complaint with the DGTR, the trade remedies authority. DGTR investigates the price and the injury, and recommends a duty. The Finance Ministry decides whether to impose it.'),
   ('Who pays it', 'The importer, at the time of clearance. The rate depends on the country and often on the producer. It is shown in a notification, usually in percent of value or a fixed amount per tonne or per unit.'),
   ('How long it lasts', 'Usually five years, with a sunset review that can extend or end it. Some are provisional first.'),
   ('How to check', ['Look up your HS code in the current anti-dumping notifications.', 'Check the country of origin and the producer name on your documents.', 'Ask your broker to confirm before you place the order.'])],
  [('Can I avoid it by changing the shipping country?', 'No. It follows the country of origin and sometimes the country of export. Rerouting can lead to an anti-circumvention case.'),
   ('Is it creditable?', 'It is a cost of the import. Only IGST is claimed as credit.'),
   ('Can it be refunded?', 'If it is later reduced or struck down, a refund claim may be possible under the rules.')],
  ('customs-duty-calculator', 'Open the duty calculator'))
g('safeguard-and-countervailing-duty', 'customs', 'Safeguard duty and countervailing duty|how they differ from anti-dumping',
  'The difference between safeguard duty, countervailing duty and anti-dumping duty, and how each affects an import into India.',
  'India has three trade remedy duties. They look alike on the bill of entry but they answer different problems.',
  [('Anti-dumping duty', 'Goods sold below a fair price by a specific foreign producer or country.'),
   ('Countervailing duty', 'Goods that a foreign government subsidised. The duty offsets the subsidy.'),
   ('Safeguard duty', 'A sudden surge of imports that harms local industry, whatever the source. It is temporary, applies to all or most countries and is reduced step by step.'),
   ('What it means for you', ['Check each of them for your HS code before you order.', 'Look at the country of origin, not only the country you ship from.', 'Check the end date: duties lapse unless extended.'])],
  [('Can all three apply to one product?', 'In theory yes, if the conditions are met. It is rare.'),
   ('Who decides?', 'DGTR investigates and recommends, and the Ministry of Finance imposes the duty.'),
   ('Where to check?', 'In the customs notifications for the product. Our calculator flags the ones in its data.')],
  ('customs-duty-calculator', 'Open the duty calculator'))
g('fta-preferential-duty-caro-tar', 'customs', 'Claiming trade agreement duty rates|FTAs and CAROTAR',
  'How to claim a lower import duty under an India trade agreement: certificate of origin, origin rules, and the CAROTAR checks.',
  'India has trade agreements with many countries, from the ASEAN group to the UAE and others. They can cut the duty to zero, but only if the goods qualify.',
  [('What you need', ['A valid preferential certificate of origin for that agreement, issued as the agreement requires.', 'Goods that meet the origin rule: for example a change of tariff heading or a minimum local content.', 'Direct shipment, or a certificate from the transit country if the cargo passes through one.']),
   ('CAROTAR', 'Customs can verify a claim under the origin rules for imports. It can ask the importer for details of the supplier cost sheet and the origin test. Keep the papers ready and ask the exporter to share them.'),
   ('If the claim fails', 'Customs can charge the full duty, with interest and sometimes a penalty. It is a good idea to check the origin rule before the order, not after.'),
   ('Check first', 'Use our trade agreement checker for the countries and the HS code. It shows the agreements and the rule family.')],
  [('Is the UAE agreement the same as the ASEAN one?', 'No. Each agreement has its own rate list and origin rules.'),
   ('Can I claim after the bill of entry?', 'Rules for refund claims are narrow. It is better to claim at the time of the bill of entry.'),
   ('Does the exporter have to help?', 'Yes. The exporter must issue the certificate and the origin proof.')],
  ('trade-agreement-preference-checker', 'Open the trade agreement checker'))
g('customs-exchange-rate-cbic-notified', 'customs', 'Customs exchange rate|how CBIC notifies it',
  'How the customs exchange rate for imports and exports is notified by CBIC, why it differs from a bank rate and where to find it.',
  'Customs does not use the bank rate. CBIC publishes its own rate for each currency, one for imports and one for exports, and it changes regularly.',
  [('How it works', 'The board notifies a list of rates for the main currencies. The import rate is used to turn the invoice into rupees for the assessable value. The export rate is used for the shipping bill.'),
   ('Which date applies', 'For an import, the rate in force on the date the bill of entry is presented, or the date set by the valuation rules. For an export, the date of the let export order or the filing, as the rule says.'),
   ('Why it matters', 'A one percent move in the rate is a one percent move in the duty base. On a large import, it matters.'),
   ('Where to find it', 'Our customs exchange rates page keeps the current notified rates.')],
  [('Is it updated daily?', 'No. It is updated regularly in a set cycle, not every day.'),
   ('Why is it different from the bank rate?', 'It is a notified rate for customs, set by the board, not a market quote.'),
   ('Which currencies are listed?', 'The main trading currencies. For others, customs uses a derived rate.')],
  ('customs-exchange-rates', 'See the customs exchange rates'))
g('hs-code-classification-general-rules', 'customs', 'HS classification|the six general rules of interpretation',
  'How to classify a product under the Harmonized System: the six General Rules of Interpretation, with simple examples.',
  'Every product has one HS code, found with six rules that are applied in order. Most classification disputes come from skipping them.',
  [('The six rules, in short', [('table', ['Rule', 'What it says'], [['1', 'The heading wording and the section and chapter notes decide first'], ['2', 'Incomplete or unassembled goods go with the complete article; mixtures follow the material rules'], ['3', 'If two headings fit, use the most specific, then the essential character, then the last in numerical order'], ['4', 'Goods that fit nowhere go to the most akin goods'], ['5', 'Cases, boxes and packing rules'], ['6', 'The same rules apply at the subheading level']])]),
   ('How to use them', ['Start with the heading text and the notes. Do not start with what a competitor used.', 'Look at what the goods are made of, what they do and how they are sold.', 'Compare the explanatory notes of the Harmonized System.']),
   ('Typical traps', ['Sets of mixed goods put up for retail.', 'Parts and accessories.', 'Goods that fit two headings, such as a machine with a software function.']),
   ('When in doubt', 'Ask for an advance ruling or take the advice of a licensed customs broker. A wrong code can be costly years later.')],
  [('Who decides the final code?', 'In India the customs officer, but the importer self assesses first, so the first responsibility is yours.'),
   ('Can I use the same code as my supplier?', 'Not blindly. The supplier code is for their country and may differ at eight digits.'),
   ('How do I find my code fast?', 'Use our HSN code finder and then check the heading notes.')],
  ('hsn-code-finder', 'Open the HSN code finder'))
g('hs-code-vs-hsn-vs-itc-hs', 'customs', 'HS code vs HSN vs ITC-HS|what is the difference',
  'The difference between HS code, HSN, ITC-HS, tariff code and SAC, and how many digits each uses in India.',
  'The same product goes by several names: HS, HSN, ITC-HS, customs tariff. They are layers of the same system.',
  [('HS code', 'Six digits. International, set by the World Customs Organization. The same in more than 200 countries.'),
   ('Customs tariff (8 digit)', 'India adds two digits to the HS code to give the eight-digit tariff line. Customs duty is set at this level.'),
   ('ITC-HS', 'DGFT\'s trade classification of the same eight digits, used for import and export policy: free, restricted, prohibited. For exports it has a separate schedule.'),
   ('HSN under GST', 'GST uses HSN codes of four, six or eight digits depending on turnover. For services, the code is the SAC.'),
   ('In practice', 'Use the eight-digit code on bills of entry and shipping bills. Use the six-digit code to talk to foreign buyers.')],
  [('How many digits does the UAE or EU use?', 'They use the same first six, with their own extra digits, up to ten for the EU.'),
   ('Can the HS code change?', 'The WCO revises it every five years or so, and India updates its tariff to match.'),
   ('Which one do I put on the invoice?', 'At least the six-digit HS code. For India, the eight-digit code is better.')],
  ('hsn-code-finder', 'Open the HSN code finder'))
g('advance-ruling-customs-india', 'customs', 'Advance rulings from Indian Customs|how to get certainty',
  'What an advance ruling on classification or valuation is, who can apply and how it protects an importer in India.',
  'An advance ruling tells you in writing, before you ship, how customs will classify or value your product. It is a way to avoid a dispute later.',
  [('What can be ruled on', 'The classification of a product, whether a notification applies to it, the origin of the goods and the valuation method, among other things.'),
   ('Who can apply', 'A person who plans to import or export, or a resident firm, within the scheme. You apply with the facts, samples or technical literature, and a fee.'),
   ('Binding effect', 'The ruling binds the applicant and the customs officers for the same facts, for the period that the rules give. It can be changed if the law changes.'),
   ('Is it worth it', 'If the import is large, repeated or the code is unclear, yes. A small one-off cargo can usually be cleared with a good broker.')],
  [('How long does it take?', 'The scheme sets a time target, but expect some months.'),
   ('Is the ruling public?', 'Rulings are published, so keep trade secrets out of your application.'),
   ('Can I appeal?', 'Yes, as the rules for the authority allow.')],
  ('hsn-code-finder', 'Open the HSN code finder'))
g('customs-demand-notice-appeal-and-refund', 'customs', 'Customs demand notice, appeal and refund|what to do',
  'What to do when you get a customs demand notice in India: show cause notice, reply, appeal, and how to claim a refund of duty.',
  'A demand from customs is not the end of the road. There is a process, with dates, and it pays to follow it.',
  [('Show cause notice', 'Before customs demands extra duty, it must usually issue a notice stating the reasons. You get a date to reply and a hearing.'),
   ('Your reply', ['Answer each allegation with documents.', 'Meet the date, or ask in writing for more time.', 'Attend the personal hearing yourself or through your representative.']),
   ('The order and the appeal', 'If you disagree with the order, you can appeal to the Commissioner (Appeals) within the time limit in the Act, and later to the tribunal. Paying a part of the duty as a pre-deposit is usually required.'),
   ('Refund', 'A refund of duty paid in excess is claimed by application within the time limit that the Act gives, with proof. Keep the bill of entry, the payment proof and the reason.')],
  [('Is there a time limit to appeal?', 'Yes, and it is short. Check the order for the date and act right away.'),
   ('Should I pay first?', 'Often a pre-deposit is needed to appeal. Your adviser will tell you the amount.'),
   ('Can a broker help?', 'A licensed customs broker or a lawyer who knows the subject can prepare the reply and the appeal.')],
  ('contact', 'Talk to our team'))
